--
-- RIVAL READINGS, SECOND FILE, EXPECTED TO FAIL. Every assertion here states what a different reading of the
-- Interpretation Act (British Columbia), RSBC 1996, c. 238 would give, and every one of them is MEANT to fail against the
-- modules, which take the reading NOTES.md (fork register) says the text supports. The first file of rival readings,
-- `bc-tests-alt-readings-expected-fail.l4`, stays at 16 failures; this one is counted separately in check.sh and must be
-- exactly 5.
-- 
-- Only a rival that changes a VALUE can be written to fail. Where the module REFUSES and the rival would give TRUE or
-- FALSE (fork F-open-list: "may do the following", "include", "may be in the following form"), an assertion of the rival's
-- value would be refused, not failed, and `check.sh` reads a refused assertion as red. Those rivals are in the fork
-- register, and the module's refusal is asserted in the module's own test file.

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `bc-interpretation-powers`
IMPORT `bc-interpretation-periods`
IMPORT `bc-interpretation-commencement`
IMPORT `bc-interpretation-reading`
IMPORT daydate

§ `Rival reading 1, F-adm: "authorized by the minister" qualifies "an assistant deputy minister" as well as "some other official" (s 23(2))`
-- On this reading an assistant deputy minister whom the minister has not authorized does not step in. The module reads the qualifier as reaching "some other official" only, so it says TRUE.
#ASSERT NOT (`s 23(2) the official has the powers and must perform the duties of the deputy minister` (`An assistant deputy minister` FALSE) TRUE)

§ `Rival reading 2, F-municipality: the exception in s 40(2) takes "municipality" out of both sources named in s 40(1)`
-- The text names "the definition of "municipality" in the Community Charter". On this reading the same word in section 1 of the Schedule to the Local Government Act is left out too. The module leaves that one in.
#ASSERT NOT (`s 40 the definition applies to the term in the enactment` `Section 1 of the Schedule to the Local Government Act` "municipality" TRUE TRUE)

§ `Rival reading 3, F-means-closed: "means any of the following" leaves the list open, as "includes" does (s 25(1), s 20(1))`
-- On this reading something that is not one of the three kinds is not shown to be NOT a reference day: the answer is a refusal. The module takes "means" as closed and says FALSE.
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 25(1) a thing a period is counted from is a reference day` `Something else`
-- Likewise an instrument that does not appoint a person to an office is not shown not to be an appointing instrument.
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 20(1) an instrument is an appointing instrument` TRUE FALSE

§ `Rival reading 4, F-always-speaking: "always speaking" does not change the day an enactment is read as of (s 7(1))`
-- On this reading a provision is read as of the day it was enacted. The module reads it as of the day it is applied.
#ASSERT `s 7(1) the day as of which an enactment is read` (YMD 1996 6 1) (YMD 2026 10 29) EQUALS YMD 1996 6 1

